WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.808 --> 00:00:01.967
[swords clanking]

00:00:05.245 --> 00:00:07.395
David Rawlings: My
goodness, this is terrible.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.760
There is so much bad in this.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:13.150
Hi, there. My name's David Rawlings,

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.330
with the London Longsword Academy.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.120
I'm a full-time swordsmanship instructor,

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.850
and today I'm gonna have
a look at a load of clips

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.392
about sword fighting and
slag them off hideously.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:32.710
So, basically what I have
with me here is a longsword.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.320
Longsword for me is generally a sword

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.680
which is held in two hands,

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.390
so it doesn't matter whether
the grip is short or long,

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.420
it doesn't really matter.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:41.440
You have a pommel, a cross
guard, a grip, a blade.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.040
Usually within fencing, we
divide that into two halves,

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.790
but different authors divide
it into different divisions.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:48.880
So, we'll have a strong half,

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.860
which is the half from
the middle to the hilt,

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.820
and then from the middle down to the point

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:53.653
is the weakest part,

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:56.971
and we're just really looking
at the geometry and leverage.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.700
You get this big thing in
historical European martial arts,

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.360
as I do, but you're not
allowed to carry swords

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.170
on your shoulder, because that's really,

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.110
really frowned upon, and
no one would ever do it.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.140
Except there's actually
critique in manuals

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.350
saying that you shouldn't do it,

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.230
which implies that people do do it.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.160
So, carrying the sword in your hand

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.130
is a very, very good thing,
and you see this a lot,

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:16.680
people holding a sword on their shoulder

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.580
and just walking around with
it without a scabbard on,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.613
necessarily, so it's ready to use.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.250
I have issues with this particular fight,

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.030
because there's a lot of reverse grip,

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:31.270
with the sword being
held backwards down here.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:34.030
It's not a good way for
you to use the sword.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.630
You sacrifice your ability to fend above,

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:37.896
very, very clearly.

00:01:37.896 --> 00:01:41.350
You have not got any reach. It's not safe.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:42.350
But he's changed grip, yay!

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:43.850
Oh, and he's back.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.000
I like how he moves.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.240
I think he's got a good
organic feel to him.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.550
Generally, the sword is
being moved in front of him,

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:52.590
which is a very, very good thing.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.310
It's very rarely just kept behind him,

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:55.540
so he's closing the space off

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.140
between him and his
opponent with the sword.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.513
One of the things I like about some of

00:01:59.513 --> 00:02:01.212
"The Witcher"'s fight
scenes is that there is

00:02:01.212 --> 00:02:03.749
this idea of moving between the opponents.

00:02:03.749 --> 00:02:05.640
And you see this in things like Godinho,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:06.860
where the idea of spinning,

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.005
something which people
really, really quite often

00:02:09.005 --> 00:02:11.030
frown upon within the HEMA community,

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.360
is very much used because
you're trying to keep opponents

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.260
away from each other, so it's
not just about fighting you

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.120
and concentrate in moving into you;

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.750
it's against driving you back

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.060
and then hitting the next person,

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.490
the next person, the next person.

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:24.800
So these spinning moves
actually become quite important,

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.050
and this angled, sinister,
straight-into-you idea

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.790
goes out the window. It's not this focus.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.940
Suddenly it's much more
wide, and it's a flurry.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.230
The thing is, is most of my
judgment on this fight scene

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:40.260
is actually Henry Cavill being
very, very good physically.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.983
And what is shown in this
fight scene being probably

00:02:43.983 --> 00:02:46.500
the most disappointing fight
scene in "The Witcher."

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.750
So, for Henry Cavill,

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.230
it's gonna have, like, an eight.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.755
For actual quality of fighting,

00:02:50.755 --> 00:02:52.380
it's gonna have about four.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.446
So, there's bits in there that actually

00:03:02.446 --> 00:03:03.690
aren't too bad, looking at it.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.580
There's evasions of the
blade from the emperor,

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.210
which could be accidental,

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.480
but you have these movements
of just moving around

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.120
as the parry's coming in.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.490
That's actually not too bad.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:14.930
You see this kind of thing quite often.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:15.763
You see it in Destreza

00:03:15.763 --> 00:03:17.690
when opponents are
moving around each other.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:19.810
Where a parry comes in,
you disengage underneath

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:22.360
as it's happening. So you have potential

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.141
for some actually quite
interesting movements in there.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:31.340
Good parry.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:34.780
You see that in Talhoffer,
usually accompanied by a wrap,

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.010
because of the closure
and the close distance.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.530
Kicking up the heels is referred
to in quite a few treaties,

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:42.670
quite often accompanied by a
movement onto the jaw as well.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.700
So potentially within this fight scene,

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.275
I'd say in those last two actions,

00:03:46.275 --> 00:03:49.341
from the cover to the kick

00:03:49.341 --> 00:03:51.180
with the legs and
sweeping up of the heels,

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.380
I'd say those are actually
quite good actions.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:56.460
Usually you'd see more control
of the opponent's weapon arm,

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.880
or their balance with
the off hand as well,

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.880
but that part's actually reasonably good.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.960
So, for that I'm actually
gonna give it a six

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.727
because I think those two
aspects save it slightly.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:15.950
OK, so, again we've got some
very, very good bits on this.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:19.860
The first bit of this is the
shunting away of the opponent.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:20.950
I really, really like this.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.350
He creates distance
between the two opponents.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:24.892
Now, you could argue that maybe he'd want

00:04:24.892 --> 00:04:26.220
to push the opponents into each other,

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.850
but he has a very, very
good instinctive response.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.070
Push one away, get the weapon from it,

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:31.970
and then on to the other one.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:33.840
And then a good clean
finish on one of the cuts.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.820
Nothing posh, just a simple
cut of wrath in effect.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:37.800
Pretty good!

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.070
Producer: Give it?
David: I'd give that a seven.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.810
I think that's quite a comfortable thing.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:43.001
Again, I think it's very easy within

00:04:43.001 --> 00:04:44.367
judging this from a HEMA perspective

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:45.930
to get very focused on an individual,

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:48.350
and this is showing
good awareness of space,

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.970
maintaining that distance,
and then simple finishes,

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.102
nothing posh.

00:04:59.342 --> 00:05:01.977
So, immediately, the
first attack is this big,

00:05:01.977 --> 00:05:03.880
I'll spin at you, I'll expose my back,

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:06.970
and the distance is so bad

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:09.136
that he could just be stabbed in the back.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:19.300
Yeah, it's a broadsword
or a saber or something,

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.870
therefore you have to go
and cut candles or scenery.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.745
You don't use this like
this. This is terrible.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:31.170
Quite often in Japanese swordsmanship,

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.710
you're moving the glass deposits,

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:34.893
the slag deposits effectively,

00:05:34.893 --> 00:05:37.220
silicon deposits that can be in steel.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.900
You're moving those
throughout the entirety

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:40.066
of the length of the blade,

00:05:40.066 --> 00:05:41.682
and through those foldings, you basically,

00:05:41.682 --> 00:05:43.030
you spread that out.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:43.980
So you make sure that your blade

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.120
hasn't got one particular weakness.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.670
Now, with European swords,
before we had that consistency,

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:51.020
we did something very similar,
except that we twisted them

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.020
like string, so that
if one thread was weak

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.430
then that could be a problem in itself,

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:57.580
but you have multiple threads,

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:58.510
and twisting them around each other,

00:05:58.510 --> 00:05:59.990
you create something stronger.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:02.870
That said, swords do snap,

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:05.390
swords do bend, and there's
always a fine point,

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:06.370
and you can't guarantee.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:07.950
The process is not perfect,

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.850
even nowadays with much
more clear definitions

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.270
of how steel should be made and produced,

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:14.103
blades still break,

00:06:14.103 --> 00:06:16.511
so it's not impossible for this to happen.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:19.810
So, this is where it
really gets quite terrible.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:22.580
There's this insane

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.420
idea that somehow longswords

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:28.050
are really heavy, clumsy weapons.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:29.070
They are not!

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:31.280
You can move a longsword much more quickly

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.970
and much more dexterously
than you can a rapier.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:36.570
It's still balanced as if
to be used in one hand,

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.690
but you have the advantage
of being able to move it

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:41.200
around this position of the forward hand.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.410
Now, even the most barbaric
moment of a longsword,

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:46.243
the barbaric technique,

00:06:46.243 --> 00:06:48.200
we have a technique called
the strike of wrath,

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.360
which is a strike that uses the entirety

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:51.940
of the anterior oblique slings.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:53.970
That's the most powerful
strike you can do,

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.720
but it's not done foolishly.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:57.030
It's done as a mechanic.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.480
It's a way of claiming
space in front of you.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:01.330
Claiming that space for yourself,

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.610
killing with it if you
can, and then going on

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:05.720
to pursue your opponent through that.

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:07.650
Wide swings don't serve anybody,

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.840
and they represent the
actual piece of equipment

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.340
very, very poorly.

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.860
Doing a spin when you're not engaged

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:17.630
with the opponent's blade is insane,

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:19.910
because you don't know
what their sword is doing.

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:21.140
And because you're so close

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:22.770
and you don't know where their sword is,

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.230
obviously they can cut you freely,

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:25.460
because they're still facing at you,

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:28.040
they still have all of
their requisite safety

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:28.943
in their hands.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:31.793
Three, four out of 10.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:39.660
This, in case you didn't know it,

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:43.040
is the best fight scene
ever in any film ever.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.240
The fencing in it is
completely irrelevant.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.190
The fencing is part of a narrative device,

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:48.750
it's not meant to overwhelm you,

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.415
it's just there, happening
while the dialogue does.

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:55.165
Inigo: You're using Bonetti's
defense against me, huh?

00:07:55.165 --> 00:07:56.190
Westley: I thought it fitting, considering

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:57.100
the rocky terrain.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.940
David: There's references
to Bonetti's defense.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:00.510
I don't even really know what that is.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:01.590
But references to Thibault,

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.530
and Thibault's one of my favorite systems,

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:05.960
but they're not showing
any awareness of Thibault.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:08.860
Thibault operates on the
idea of the perfect angle

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:10.600
that you see in a lot
of Spanish swordsmanship

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:12.040
and destreza particularly,

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:14.060
here where you have a 90-degree angle,

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:15.860
which is maintained as much as possible.

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:18.300
And you don't make lateral
movements with your arms.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:20.190
But all of this is what
we call vulgar angles,

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:21.990
where the point is higher than the hilt,

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:23.490
and lots of lateral movement.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:24.323
Inigo: Naturally.

00:08:24.323 --> 00:08:26.712
You must expect me to
attack with Capo Ferro.

00:08:26.712 --> 00:08:27.545
Westley: Naturally.

00:08:27.545 --> 00:08:29.680
But I find that Thibault
cancels out Capo Ferro.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:31.540
David: Rapier, some people
will call it a side sword.

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:33.320
The divisions are really a later thing.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:34.520
We don't worry about it too much.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:36.210
It's a sword that you hold in one hand.

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:37.880
Now, again, we have the quillons.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:40.160
Quite often, or it could
just be like the cross guard

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:42.750
in effect, quite often
people would hold this

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.140
simply like so without
their finger over the bars,

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:47.400
and these extra rings would be to stop

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.070
things from landing on
your fingers when you're

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.220
holding the sword normally.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:51.800
And this allows you to
put your thumb on there.

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:53.338
Later on, you start seeing people

00:08:53.338 --> 00:08:55.265
holding the sword like so. OK?

00:08:55.265 --> 00:08:56.406
But it's not universal.

00:08:56.406 --> 00:08:59.076
Sometimes it is just used like this. OK?

00:08:59.076 --> 00:09:02.460
Sometimes these are very, very
ornate, as you can see here,

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.370
there's a lot of wire around here.

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:06.230
And then sometimes,
it's just a simple cup.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:07.830
Westley: There's something
I ought to tell you.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:08.663
Inigo: Tell me.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:11.294
Westley: I'm not left-handed either.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:17.610
David: The idea of being
able to fence with both hands

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.498
shouldn't be necessarily a problem.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:22.970
Fighting somebody who is
differently handed to you

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:25.210
as well shouldn't be
an uncommon experience,

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:27.530
so this kind of thing,
although it's amusing,

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:29.800
you don't want to do this sort
of thing at close distance,

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:30.968
because obviously while you're busy

00:09:30.968 --> 00:09:32.267
changing your sword hand,

00:09:32.267 --> 00:09:34.183
your opponent can stab you
and this kind of thing.

00:09:34.183 --> 00:09:35.860
Here, you have a bit where
as the blade comes through,

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:37.765
it disengages, so you're
having the blade changing

00:09:37.765 --> 00:09:39.020
from one side to the other.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.010
And in order to fight
that, what you're doing is,

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.060
rather than doing lateral
parryings like this,

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.180
you're wrapping the blade and
you're staying engaged to it.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:46.528
And that can be used to throw

00:09:46.528 --> 00:09:47.753
the opponent's sword out their hands.

00:09:47.753 --> 00:09:49.660
So there's a degree of reality in that.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:50.960
It's good.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:53.350
Technically wise, I'd
still give it sort of like

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:54.370
a six, seven.

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:57.350
Even with its parody
fencing, it still has better

00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:00.100
fencing than a lot of the others.

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:12.480
That's quite nice, because
in effect, you have a very,

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:15.280
very nasty version of a
movement of conclusion.

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:18.230
This is basically where
in using a sword to parry,

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:20.500
here we then do something
with the off hand.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.340
If we were doing Verdadera,
the idea would be to

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:24.780
restrain the opponent's weapon
hand so it can control it.

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:26.680
And we can choose
whether they live or die,

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:28.570
and we can show mercy at this point.

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:31.060
If you're doing this, this is lovely.

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:32.790
He doesn't have to worry
about the guy's off hand

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:35.080
so much, because the guy
has both hands on the sword,

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:37.000
there's a closure off of the line,

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:38.980
and the guy giving him the distance.

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:40.200
Because he doesn't have to move his feet.

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:41.830
The guy's gonna come and hit him anyway.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:42.830
He gets to push it aside.

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:44.040
He gets to thrust through.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:46.330
He's got control of the
opponent's sword, still,

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:47.230
and he gets to finish him.

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:48.440
So that, I actually quite like.

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:50.379
That's pretty good.

00:10:50.379 --> 00:10:51.470
I'm not entirely sure

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:53.180
holding your hand palm
down with a longsword

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:54.750
is a good way not to get the sword taken.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:56.866
Oh, my goodness, this is terrible.

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:00.430
There is so much bad in this.

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:02.148
Again, we're back on this idea that

00:11:02.148 --> 00:11:04.730
if you've got a longsword,
you have to do this with it,

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:06.240
and it has to be wide and spinny,

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.610
and it has to be these big moves.

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:08.670
It's meant to be dexterous.

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:11.220
You have this idea that
there's basically half an el.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:13.240
The idea of being this kind of space here,

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:14.550
rather halfway between here and here.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:16.020
You don't wanna be more than this way

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:18.560
far away from your opponent
with the point at all times,

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:20.670
so if they drive you away,
you're trying to find a way

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:22.140
to get your point onto them.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:24.923
Most longsword is intrinsically

00:11:24.923 --> 00:11:26.531
trying to dominate a space

00:11:26.531 --> 00:11:28.840
very, very close into the
corner of your opponent.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:30.340
If they move you sufficiently around,

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.380
you're immediately back
at another opening.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:35.140
If they stay tight on the
blade and the angle's tight,

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:36.900
just enough to parry
like so, you're trying to

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:39.490
drive the blade in and still
maintain a contact on there.

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:42.973
All this wide-around movement
is complete nonsense.

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:48.020
OK, knocking the blade
down and striking back up.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:50.215
Now, you could argue that this is almost

00:11:50.215 --> 00:11:51.873
a nodding, in effect.

00:11:51.873 --> 00:11:53.280
The idea of beating someone's blade down

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.349
and then hitting back
up with the false edge.

00:11:57.007 --> 00:11:58.780
Here you see where the
sword is sweeping wide

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:00.490
and one person is
standing with their point

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:01.440
right in front of the other.

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:03.140
You don't parry then come back here.

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:05.060
You stand here, and you
stab them immediately,

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:07.227
because the blade has
moved out of presence.

00:12:08.959 --> 00:12:09.959
Well, that was good.

00:12:09.959 --> 00:12:12.230
Yep, if someone has got their
back you and you have a spear,

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:13.660
do stab them in the back of the leg.

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:15.394
That, again, redeeming feature,

00:12:15.394 --> 00:12:17.161
that's given it another point.

00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:23.669
So, I quite like the fight scene between

00:12:23.669 --> 00:12:25.290
Brienne of Tarth and Arya.

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:26.220
I think that's quite good.

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:27.630
It's cohesive.

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:29.590
It has that real focus to it

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:30.940
so the movement is constant.

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:33.130
Neither of them is really sacrificing.

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:34.430
There's no great pauses.

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:36.170
There's always, I want
to be back in the fight.

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:37.545
I want to be back in it.

00:12:42.508 --> 00:12:45.135
I do like, in general, the Hound fights

00:12:45.135 --> 00:12:47.550
because they are just,
like, eh, pff, dead.

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:50.710
That's good, and I think
having that idea that

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:53.560
a blade is intrinsically
there to either stab someone

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:55.770
or hit them with it and
remove all the, sort of, like,

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:58.495
fussy detail is a very good
thing to have in a series.

00:13:10.778 --> 00:13:12.820
First, the twirling is dreadful.

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:14.810
But the blades are all over the place.

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:15.910
There's no safety in them.

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:18.610
And now, being focused
on a single opponent,

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:21.118
we could use the sword in
a much more efficient way.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:27.020
Even if I were to twirl
the swords towards you,

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:29.120
you'd always want one blade in presence,

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:31.380
always one blade doing something.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:33.538
Never having the swords over here.

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:38.760
At that point, I think
the idea of restraining

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:40.682
two weapon hands becomes more important

00:13:40.682 --> 00:13:42.830
than actually taking the
weapons out of the hand.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:44.700
So at that point, I would suggest that,

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:46.330
potentially, you're
looking at a way of either

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:48.360
throwing the opponent as
far from you as possible.

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:50.250
Throwing them down the really big hole

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:52.037
on the other side of you
might be a good thing.

00:13:52.037 --> 00:13:53.777
Producer: Do you have a
mark out of 10 for that one?

00:13:53.777 --> 00:13:55.027
David: Uh, two.

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:05.840
Yep, she's dead now.

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:08.510
Yeah, you're really looking
at just absolute theater here.

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:09.560
And that's fine.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:10.750
It is what it is.

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:13.990
But it's terrible,
terrible sword fighting.

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:16.440
This idea of driving
people apart to fight;

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:18.010
so, you don't want to be standing still.

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:20.160
You don't want people
to be managing the space

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:22.344
while your space is getting
more and more cramped.

00:14:26.506 --> 00:14:29.150
One of the things that is
misunderstood quite often

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:30.505
is that in pattern-welded blades,

00:14:30.505 --> 00:14:32.690
quite often these were
given mirror polishes.

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:35.930
And I believe, unless this is anecdotal,

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:37.700
that there's reference to
people blowing on the blades,

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:39.650
so that the breath actually brings out

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:42.150
the pattern weld for a
moment, so you can see it.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:44.662
So, yeah, blades
historically are incredibly

00:14:44.662 --> 00:14:47.090
highly polished in
various different places.

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:48.580
Certainly in Japanese
swords, that's a thing,

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:50.246
certainly European swords, it's a thing.

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:55.760
Interesting tactical decisions here.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:57.240
So you have a point where
somebody has control

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.530
of the blade, and if you think,
like, of parrying daggers,

00:14:59.530 --> 00:15:01.786
you have really options to

00:15:01.786 --> 00:15:05.036
take the blade of the
opponent on your dagger

00:15:05.036 --> 00:15:06.136
or to take them on your sword

00:15:06.136 --> 00:15:07.630
and to attack them with the other.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:10.630
But in order to do so, you'd
want to maintain that contact.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:12.830
So to do an action like
this, with the opponent's

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:14.360
blade still directly in the middle,

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:16.120
doesn't make any sense, really.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:18.630
So you had a point of control
or a point of control,

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:21.120
both of those have been sacrificed.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.380
It's amazing that one of
these people didn't die

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:24.290
in the process of this.

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:25.793
There should've either been disemboweling

00:15:25.793 --> 00:15:27.160
or a stab or whichever.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:28.880
Or the other person should've
plowed straight through

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:30.284
the middle in the space.

00:15:30.284 --> 00:15:33.660
From the voting viewpoint
of a good cohesive

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:35.960
fight scene that involves
realistic technique,

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:37.519
I'm gonna give that two.

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:47.293
OK, the first miracle of this fight is

00:15:47.293 --> 00:15:49.520
they don't manage to kill each other.

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:51.500
There are forms which
consist about fighting

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:53.140
around somebody.

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:54.894
Usually that other person is disabled

00:15:54.894 --> 00:15:56.422
so that they're kind of in a position

00:15:56.422 --> 00:15:58.163
where you don't have to worry about them.

00:15:58.163 --> 00:15:59.740
They'll be prone or something like this.

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:01.440
Or perhaps you'll be bodyguarding someone,

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:03.390
so it's a way of moving around each other.

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:06.788
I would not really like
to have my back to someone

00:16:06.788 --> 00:16:08.813
who is swinging something
sharp or burning.

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:15.519
So much of this is almost a method

00:16:15.519 --> 00:16:18.230
to show how he feels.

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:20.840
So you have an expression
of emotion going on

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:23.438
in the fight scenes, rather
than necessarily being

00:16:23.438 --> 00:16:25.390
this very, very tight, cohesive fight.

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:27.340
His anger is always explicit.

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:29.810
Everything is these wide, powerful slashes

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.452
that aren't necessarily good for defense.

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:36.450
It's so heavily stylized.

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:40.410
I think in things like
the "Revenge of the Sith,"

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:42.617
you have these very,
very dynamic fight scenes

00:16:42.617 --> 00:16:45.670
between Obi-Wan and
Anakin, and those, I think,

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:46.910
are much more expressive at combat,

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:48.400
even if the distance
is a little bit funny.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:49.590
Even if they're standing toe-to-toe

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:50.800
and waving the swords around.

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:54.590
There's a lot more aggression
and interaction in that.

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:56.830
As such, I think I'd
give it a five out of 10.

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:59.950
As in, it has aspects that I really enjoy.

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:01.840
If I was going for my
enjoyment of the fight scene,

00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:03.148
it would be an eight.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.410
I really, really love
the Yoda fight scenes.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:08.552
I always have.

00:17:09.527 --> 00:17:10.880
Again, it's that point

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:13.280
where you separate any desire for reality.

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:14.320
It doesn't have to be real.

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:15.673
You know, if it's got dragons,

00:17:15.673 --> 00:17:17.250
if it's got the force, it doesn't matter.

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:19.570
It has to be something expressive

00:17:19.570 --> 00:17:21.095
of all the things you want in there.

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:31.193
Yes, absolutely.

00:17:31.193 --> 00:17:32.740
If you can cut flesh, you can cut fabric.

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:35.448
It's, again, it depends
on different things,

00:17:35.448 --> 00:17:36.760
like how sharp the sword is.

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:38.910
But they are made to cut things.

00:17:39.944 --> 00:17:41.275
The horses were very realistic.

00:17:41.275 --> 00:17:42.398
Let's give it a one.

00:17:42.398 --> 00:17:43.348
[producer laughing]

